1. Product Overview
1.1 Product Vision

The IT Expense & Resource Management System is a centralized web application designed to manage, monitor, record, categorize and analyze all IT department expenses, purchases, invoices, subscriptions, assets and inventory.

The application will replace the existing manual Excel-based expense tracking process with a structured digital management system backed by MongoDB.

The application must maintain a single centralized source of truth while providing separate live pages for individual expense categories.

For example, when a new Courier Labels invoice is uploaded:

The system extracts the invoice information.
It identifies the relevant products.
It categorizes the transaction.
It stores the transaction in MongoDB.
It displays the purchase in the Courier Labels page.
It updates the centralized transaction ledger.
It updates stock quantities when the goods are received.
It updates the vendor's purchase history.
It updates the dashboard and financial reports.
It makes the transaction available for Excel export.

Users must not need to enter the same information multiple times.

1.2 Primary Objectives
Replace manual expense spreadsheets with a centralized application.
Maintain individual pages for different expense categories.
Store all records in MongoDB.
Automatically categorize uploaded invoices and bills.
Calculate GST, totals and currency conversions.
Maintain purchase, payment and approval histories.
Track consumables and fixed assets.
Manage recurring subscriptions and renewal dates.
Provide real-time dashboards and reports.
Import existing Excel records.
Export reports in the original Excel format and a professional format.
Support secure online and local access.
Maintain complete audit history.
1.3 Core Product Principle

One database, one transaction record, multiple live views.

The application must not create separate, duplicated expense records for different category pages.

Each category page, master ledger, dashboard and report must retrieve the appropriate information from the same underlying MongoDB transactions.

