30. Business Rules and Important Data Integrity Decisions

These rules are mandatory.

One purchase must have one canonical transaction identity.
Category pages must be filtered views of shared records.
A multi-item invoice must have one invoice record and multiple linked line items.
Approval status, payment status and goods-received status must be independent.
Approved purchases must not automatically increase inventory.
Inventory increases only when goods are received.
Expected recurring expenses must not be counted as actual paid expenses.
Historical exchange rates must not be overwritten.
Historical invoice values must be preserved.
GST must be configurable and must not be assumed to be 18% for all purchases.
Manual invoice amounts must not be silently changed by automatic calculations.
Historical data corrections must be auditable.
Deletion of completed financial transactions must use an authorized reversal or cancellation process.
Excel exports must not duplicate transactions.
Dashboard totals must use defined financial reporting rules.
Every transaction must retain its source invoice and vendor reference when available.
Imported historical expenses must retain their original approval and payment information.
31. Initial Data Migration

The existing expense spreadsheet supplied for this project will be used as the initial historical dataset.

The migration should:

Import all valid historical transactions.
Preserve original amounts.
Preserve invoice numbers.
Preserve vendor names.
Preserve dates.
Preserve approval information.
Map products to the appropriate categories.
Identify recurring services.
Identify hardware purchases.
Identify consumable purchases.
Flag ambiguous records.

The original spreadsheet should remain available as the source document.

The separate category-page examples supplied by the user should be treated as desired presentation formats and product tracking requirements, not as an independent source of additional purchases.

If category sample data conflicts with the original transaction spreadsheet, the application must flag the conflict instead of adding duplicate purchases.

A reconciliation report must compare:

Original transaction count.
Imported transaction count.
Original total amounts.
Imported total amounts.
Missing records.
Duplicate records.
Unresolved discrepancies.
